AVI container test
==================

The RIFF/AVI layout is hand-written by VideoRecorder, and every historical bug
in it came from hand-counted byte offsets drifting out of sync with the bytes
actually emitted. AviFormat.h therefore derives all offsets from the chunk sizes
and keeps no Arduino dependencies, so the same code the firmware runs can be
compiled and exercised on a PC.

avi_format_test.cpp checks the derived offsets, then muxes real JPEGs into an
AVI using the same calls as VideoRecorder::recordVideo(). ffprobe and ffmpeg
judge the result. This catches malformed containers that on-device testing tends
to miss, because players are forgiving enough to play a subtly broken file.

Requires: g++, ffmpeg/ffprobe. No hardware, no ESP32 toolchain.

Run
---

    cd test
    OUT=$(mktemp -d)

    # 30 frames = 3 seconds at 10 fps
    mkdir -p "$OUT/frames"
    ffmpeg -v error -f lavfi -i testsrc=size=640x480:rate=10:duration=3 \
           -q:v 10 "$OUT/frames/f%03d.jpg"

    g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -Wextra -I ../lib/VideoRecorder/src \
        -o "$OUT/avi_test" avi_format_test.cpp

    "$OUT/avi_test" "$OUT/out.avi" "$OUT"/frames/*.jpg

Then verify the container. All three must pass:

    # 1. No demuxer warnings, and the metadata must be exact
    ffprobe -v error -select_streams v:0 \
      -show_entries stream=nb_frames,r_frame_rate,width,height,codec_name \
      -show_entries format=duration -of default=nw=1 "$OUT/out.avi"
    #   expect: mjpeg, 640x480, r_frame_rate=10/1, nb_frames=30, duration=3.000000

    # 2. Every frame decodes; any output at all means corruption
    ffmpeg -v error -i "$OUT/out.avi" -f null -

    # 3. Frames decoded matches frames written
    ffprobe -v error -count_frames -select_streams v:0 \
      -show_entries stream=nb_read_frames -of csv=p=0 "$OUT/out.avi"

Note on strictness
------------------

ffmpeg's AVI demuxer is tolerant and will rescan its way past malformed chunk
sizes, so "it plays in VLC" is not evidence the container is correct. The
original header understated nothing visibly, yet a spec-literal parser
following the declared LIST hdrl size landed 8 bytes past the movi LIST and
mis-parsed. If you change the layout, verify with a parser that trusts the
declared sizes rather than one that recovers from them.
